            <ArticleTitle>Stearoyl-CoA Desaturase 1 (SCD1) is a key factor mediating diabetes in MyD88-deficient mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>340-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.gene.2012.01.024</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Saturated fatty acids, acting as ligands for toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), induce inflammation and mediate the development of insulin resistance. Myeloid differentiation factor 88 (MyD88) is an adaptor protein for TLR4. Previously, we found MyD88-deficient mice fed a high-fat diet (HFD) exhibited a severe diabetic phenotype. Stearoyl-CoA Desaturase 1 (SCD1) is the rate-limiting enzyme in the biosynthesis of monounsaturated fatty acids and known as a risk factor of diabetes. In the present study, we found SCD1 was dramatically increased in HFD-fed MyD88-deficient mice liver. This finding showed the novel linkage between MyD88 and SCD1 in the development of diabetes mellitus.</AbstractText>
